# BUGS.md section 31 -- FIXED 2026-08-27, both halves.
#
# (a) Transitive imports now survive the flat vendor tree. A vendored
# module's own mod.aon used to stop the upward walk for the store, so
# `@"corp.example/schemas/common@1"` -- imported by service@1, which is
# itself imported from here -- resolved against
# aontu_meta/vendor/corp.example/schemas/service@1/ and found no aontu_meta/vendor/
# there, failing `module not fetched` even though common@1 IS vendored
# flat beside it. The resolver now walks to EVERY enclosing mod.aon
# root (projectRoots in ts/src/mod.ts, go/mod.go) and tries each root's
# store and lockfile in turn, so the flat layout `mod vendor` produces
# is the layout a nested import reads -- and the workaround (nesting a
# second aontu_meta/vendor/ inside the dependency) is no longer needed, which
# matters because `mod manifest` strips aontu_meta/vendor/ from the layer.
#
# (b) `tidy` no longer pins a module it cannot evaluate. It used to lock
# canon aon1-XaOkx_EXlEJ1tMhinEkWQDYl1aSmVzoB7LA_Dp0u2-Y -- the
# canon-hash of `nil`, identical for ANY module whose import fails, so
# the pin carried no information and two different broken modules
# locked the same pin. Tidy now refuses (verdict: error, exit 4, no
# lockfile written) with the same message `aontu hash` gives the same
# file: "does not evaluate on its own; nothing to pin".
#
# Pinned by ts/test/mod.test.ts and go/modtool_test.go
# (TestModTransitiveVendorResolves, TestModTidyRefusesAnUnevaluableModule).
# run: aontu mod tidy . && aontu main.aon
# expected: verdict: ok, both modules pinned; {"svc":{"name":"checkout","port":8080}} exit 0
# actual:   verdict: ok, both modules pinned; {"svc":{"name":"checkout","port":8080}} exit 0
mod: { path: "corp.example/checkout", main: "main.aon" }
dep: {
  "corp.example/schemas/service@1": { v: "1.4.2" }
  "corp.example/schemas/common@1": { v: "1.0.0" }
}
